00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:51,100
Okay so I think there's a misunderstanding of what Kisquale does because if you went and looked up Kisquale it is not an aromatase inhibitor.

00:00:51,100 --> 00:01:02,100
So it is for her to positive breast cancer but it blocks a different pathway what's called the CDK pathway.

00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:10,100
So it's called a CDK inhibitor. It's not an aromatase inhibitor.

00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:27,100
So most people that are on Kisquale are also on an aromatase inhibitor and most people that are following their oncologists' suggestions are taking a medically prescribed aromatase inhibitor.

00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:35,100
Not dim or aromaclear or something they're taking the drug that is the aromatase inhibitor.

00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:50,100
So if let's say I was on dim or some natural aromatase inhibitor which again is going to do equally to what the drug will just shut off all astrogens.

00:01:50,100 --> 00:02:07,100
And then I was going to do Kisquale. I wouldn't go off the aromatase inhibitor either the drug or dim because Kisquale isn't taking the place of a natural or medically prescribed aromatase inhibitor.

00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:17,100
The answer to the question yes I would go back on dim if that's what you were using for your aromatase inhibite.

00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:20,100
Definitely I would go back on that.

